WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.330
Okay, so I've talked before about how the
program is constantly iterating and changing

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:09.719
and I want to be improving the show and making
the program reflective, not only of what I

00:00:09.719 --> 00:00:13.240
think are important political issues, but
making the format reflective of what people

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.659
in the audience enjoy.

00:00:14.659 --> 00:00:20.490
And so we had some questions that I asked
last week about interview duration and story

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.880
duration, number of stories per day, live
calls versus voicemails.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.119
And I have the results now hundreds and hundreds
of people emailed me.

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:32.430
Some of the trends are very clear and on some
questions the audience seems kind of undecided

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.030
or at least in different, we could say.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:38.399
So here are the results from what we asked
you about last week.

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:43.300
First of all, should interviews be reduced
in number and extended in length?

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.550
This was the most overwhelming result.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:50.110
73% of the audience wants fewer interviews
that are longer.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.570
Um, 27% does not want that change.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:55.760
So that's a really significant, uh, a result.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.170
Should voicemails be replaced with live calls?

00:00:58.170 --> 00:00:59.170
The answer is no.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:01.340
80% of the audience says no, no, no.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.520
Keep the voicemails and don't replace them
with live calls.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:06.730
Should we cover more stories per show?

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:09.370
It's almost a 50, 50 split, 45%.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:13.640
Say cover more stories per show, 55% say don't.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.570
And then should each story be longer in duration?

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:19.750
Almost two thirds of you said yes.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.680
Extend the stories a bit, a little more than
one third said no.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.630
So, uh, some pretty significant numbers there
at least as far as people are going in the

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.550
direction of preferring longer form content.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:32.830
That seems to be the trend.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:38.290
Wanting interviews to be longer and fewer
in number wanting stories to be longer and

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:41.390
roughly the same in number based on these
results.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:46.570
Some other common requests or ideas that came
from people who uh, got in touch.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:51.170
One idea was canceled the Friday classic interviews,
uh, saying, listen, David, those interviews

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:52.700
are available on the youtube channel.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.911
It can sometimes be interesting to sort of
like revive, revive an old interview, but

00:01:56.911 --> 00:01:57.911
we can find them.

00:01:57.911 --> 00:02:00.100
You could probably do something better with
that time.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:05.230
Also a lot of requests for more of our deep
dive long forms segments, which is something

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:06.590
we're considering.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:11.260
And then also lots of ideas about what I should
be doing when we launch on twitch.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:16.780
The David Pakman show is in very advanced
discussions with twitch and their partner

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:17.780
team.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:21.520
Uh, and some of the ideas that people came
up with used twitch just to take audience

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.530
calls and questions, pull some of that stuff
off of the youtube channel, do it on twitch,

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:30.519
uh, play chess, uh, presumably against members
